    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`, and all 4 tests pass (`pytest -q`, 0.69s).\n\n**How it works:** it opens `mcp.Client(server, mode=\"legacy\")`. That runs your server object in the same process and connects to it through in-memory streams, so there's no subprocess and no sockets. With `mode=\"legacy\"` the client does the same `initialize` handshake and sends the same JSON-RPC messages that `check_command` uses over stdio. It then calls `client.session.call_tool(...)` for each case in order and runs the results through the existing `compare`. Mismatches come back in the same order and form as `check_command`.\n\nI set `mode=\"legacy\"` on purpose. By default, the SDK's `Client` handles an in-process server differently: it skips JSON-RPC and the handshake entirely, so its results could differ from what the stdio path produces.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_inproc.py`. It builds a real server with the SDK's decorator API (`MCPServer` plus `@server.tool()`) and checks two things:\n- the example cases (`add` and `greet`) produce no mismatches;\n- a list with failing and passing cases returns the right mismatches, with what the tool actually returned, in case order.\n\n**Docs lookup:** CLAUDE.md asks for the API to be checked in Context7 first, but Context7 returned \"Monthly quota exceeded\". Instead I read the installed mcp 2.2.0 source in `.venv`, mainly `mcp/client/client.py`, `mcp/client/_memory.py` and `mcp/client/session.py`.\n\nNo dependencies were added or changed."
  },
